# Part 2A: Q61 to Q120
REMAINING_QS_P1 = {
    61: ("Polymorphic Transformations: Enantiotropic vs. Monotropic Inversions", "Polymorphic transformations which can proceed reversibly in either direction at a definite transition temperature and pressure are described as :", {"A": "Monotropic", "B": "Metamict", "C": "Allotropic", "D": "Enantiotropic (e.g. alpha-quartz <-> beta-quartz, calcite <-> aragonite)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 102–104); Putnis (Mineral Sciences)"),
    62: ("Polymorphic Inversion Volume: Density and Expansion from Stishovite to Quartz", "In the reconstructive polymorphic transformation of high-density Stishovite or Coesite to Low-Quartz at crustal pressures, the lattice undergoes a :", {"A": "Massive volumetric expansion (+50% to +100% volume increase, density drops from 4.28 to 2.65 g/cm3)", "B": "Volumetric contraction", "C": "Zero volume change", "D": "Decrease in entropy"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *62, p. 199); Sam Boggs Jr.; Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    63: ("Crystal Chemistry: Diadochy Definition in Mineral Lattices", "'Diadochy' in crystal chemistry and geochemistry is precisely defined as :", {"A": "The complete mechanical separation of phases", "B": "The growth of one mineral on another with crystallographic continuity", "C": "The capacity of different chemical elements or ions to replace one another in a crystal lattice site (ionic substitution)", "D": "The alteration of a mineral by hydration"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 106); V.M. Goldschmidt"),
    64: ("Mineral Habit: Acicular vs. Filiform Habits in Zeolites and Asbestos", "Minerals that crystallize in extremely slender, needle-like crystals (such as Natrolite or Sillimanite) are described as having a/an :", {"A": "Tabular habit", "B": "Acicular habit (needle-like)", "C": "Botryoidal habit", "D": "Dendritic habit"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 20); Perkins"),
    65: ("Botryoidal Habit: Globular Aggregates in Hematite and Chalcedony", "Mineral aggregates resembling a compact cluster of grapes with smooth rounded hemispherical surfaces are termed :", {"A": "Reniform (kidney-shaped)", "B": "Mammillary", "C": "Botryoidal (grape-like aggregates, e.g. hematite, chalcedony, psilomelane)", "D": "Stalactitic"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 21); Nesse"),
    66: ("Fibrous Habit: Chrysotile Asbestos Flexible Fibers", "The serpentine variety that famously occurs in silky, easily separable, highly flexible fibrous bundles utilized as commercial asbestos is :", {"A": "Chrysotile (clinochrysotile)", "B": "Antigorite (platy/lamellar)", "C": "Lizardite (massive)", "D": "Tremolite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 344–352); Klein & Dutrow (p. 508)"),
    67: ("Twinning Types: Contact Twins vs. Penetration Twins", "A crystal twin where two individual halves are joined along a single well-defined planar composition surface is a :", {"A": "Penetration twin (e.g. Staurolite cross, Fluorite interpenetration)", "B": "Contact twin (e.g. Spinel law, Japanese twin)", "C": "Polysynthetic twin", "D": "Cyclic twin"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 188–192); F.C. Phillips"),
    68: ("Ring Silicate Geometric Limits: Instability of 2-Membered Tetrahedral Rings", "Two-membered silicate tetrahedral rings [Si2O6]4- do not occur in nature because :", {"A": "A two-membered ring requires severe, unfeasible distortion of the Si-O-Si bond angle (<90°) and brings highly charged Si4+ cations into extreme electrostatic repulsion", "B": "Oxygen atoms cannot share electrons", "C": "Silicon ions are too large", "D": "Aluminum replaces silicon completely"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *68, p. 199); Klein & Dutrow (p. 445); Linus Pauling (1929)"),
    69: ("Gemstone Hardness: Beryl Hardness on the Mohs Scale", "The gemstone mineral Beryl (Be3Al2Si6O18), including varieties Emerald and Aquamarine, has a diagnostic Mohs hardness of :", {"A": "5.0 to 5.5", "B": "6.0 to 6.5", "C": "7.0 (Quartz)", "D": "7.5 to 8.0 (scratches quartz easily, scratched by topaz/corundum)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 28, 446); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    70: ("Physical Property Matching: Diagnostic Testing Protocols", "Match the diagnostic test/property with its corresponding mineral application :\n(A) Magnetic attraction ——— (1) Magnetite\n(B) Effervescence with cold dilute HCl — (2) Calcite\n(C) Salty taste ——————— (3) Halite\n(D) Feel soapy/greasy ————— (4) Talc", {"A": "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4", "B": "A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3", "C": "A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3", "D": "A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, Ch. 2, pp. 34–38); Perkins"),
    71: ("Anisotropic Hardness: Kyanite Cleavage and Hardness Orientation", "The mineral Kyanite exhibits dramatic hardness variation because its hardness parallel to the length of the bladed crystal (c-axis) is 4.5, whereas across the blade it is :", {"A": "6.5 to 7.0 (due to dense packing of silicate chains across the blade)", "B": "1.0 to 2.0", "C": "9.0 to 10.0", "D": "Zero"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 72–75); Klein & Dutrow (p. 504)"),
    72: ("Metamict State: Radiation Damage in Zircon and Thorite", "A mineral whose internal crystalline lattice has been disrupted and rendered amorphous by alpha-particle bombardment from radioactive uranium/thorium decay while retaining external crystal form is termed :", {"A": "Pseudomorph", "B": "Metamict (e.g. metamict zircon, allanite, thorite)", "C": "Paramorph", "D": "Amorphous glass"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *72, p. 199); Klein & Dutrow (p. 182); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    73: ("Carbonate Staining: Meigen's Test to Distinguish Calcite from Aragonite", "Meigen's chemical reaction (boiling powdered mineral in cobalt nitrate solution, producing a lilac/violet precipitate on aragonite) is utilized to distinguish :", {"A": "Dolomite from Magnesite", "B": "Siderite from Rhodochrosite", "C": "Calcite (remains white/takes slow stain) from Aragonite (rapidly turns lilac-pink)", "D": "Apatite from Fluorite"}, "Hutchison (Laboratory Handbook of Petrographic Techniques); Klein & Dutrow (p. 411)"),
    74: ("Archaic Mineral Nomenclature: The Miner's Term 'Glance'", "The traditional historical mining term 'glance' (as in 'lead glance', 'copper glance', 'silver glance') refers to minerals possessing :", {"A": "Transparent adamantine brilliance", "B": "Metallic luster and dark streak (e.g. Galena = lead glance, Chalcocite = copper glance, Argentite = silver glance)", "C": "Vitreous glassy luster", "D": "Earthy clay-like texture"}, "Dana's System of Mineralogy; Klein & Dutrow"),
    75: ("Mineral Cleavage Diagnostics: Galena vs. Sphalerite", "Galena (PbS) displays 3 perfect cleavages intersecting at 90° (cubic cleavage {001}), whereas Sphalerite (ZnS) displays :", {"A": "6 directions of perfect dodecahedral cleavage {011} at 60° and 90°", "B": "4 directions of octahedral cleavage", "C": "3 directions of rhombohedral cleavage", "D": "1 direction of basal cleavage"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 30, 361); Nesse"),
    76: ("Specific Gravity Calculation: Heavy Liquid Suspensions", "When a mineral grain neither sinks nor floats but remains suspended indefinitely in a heavy liquid of known density rho_liquid, the specific gravity of the mineral is :", {"A": "Much greater than rho_liquid", "B": "Much less than rho_liquid", "C": "Zero", "D": "Exactly equal to rho_liquid (Archimedes' principle equilibrium)", "E": "Undefined"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *76, p. 199); Tutton; Klein & Dutrow (p. 34)"),
    77: ("Radioactivity Detection: Scintillation and Geiger Counter Standards", "The presence of uranium and thorium in radioactive minerals such as Uraninite (Pitchblende) and Monazite is detected by their emission of :", {"A": "Infrared thermal rays", "B": "Ionizing radiation (alpha, beta particles and gamma rays detected by Geiger-Muller or scintillation counters)", "C": "Visible phosphorescent sparks", "D": "Microwaves"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 38); Faure (Principles of Isotope Geology)"),
    78: ("Mineral Fracture: Hackly vs. Splintery in Native Metals", "The jagged, sharp, tearing fracture typical of malleable native metals like Native Copper, Gold, and Silver is termed :", {"A": "Conchoidal fracture", "B": "Hackly fracture", "C": "Fibrous fracture", "D": "Even fracture"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 31); Perkins"),
    79: ("Mineral Magnetism: Curie Temperature of Magnetite", "Magnetite (Fe3O4) loses its spontaneous ferromagnetism and becomes paramagnetic upon heating above its Curie temperature, which is approximately :", {"A": "100°C", "B": "350°C", "C": "580°C (Curie point of pure magnetite)", "D": "1200°C"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 35); Stacey & Banerjee (The Physical Principles of Rock Magnetism)"),
    80: ("Silicate Structures: Sheet Silicates with Hexagonal Tetrahedral Meshes", "In phyllosilicates (sheet silicates, [Si2O5]2-), three oxygens of each tetrahedron are shared to form planar infinite sheets with characteristic :", {"A": "Tetragonal 4-fold meshes", "B": "Orthorhombic rectangular meshes", "C": "Linear single chains", "D": "Hexagonal pseudo-hexagonal 6-fold ring meshes"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 488–492); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    81: ("Inosilicate Cleavage: Relation to Tetrahedral Chains", "The cleavage angles of 87° in pyroxenes and 56° in amphiboles are directly controlled by the :", {"A": "Size of divalent calcium cations", "B": "Stacking of octahedral layers only", "C": "Presence of interlayer water molecules", "D": "I-beam structural units: cleavage cuts between strong I-beams avoiding breaking strong Si-O bonds within the silicate chains"}, "J.B. Thompson Jr. (1978, Biopyriboles and polysomatic series); Klein & Dutrow (pp. 458–460)"),
    82: ("Zeolite Group Properties: Continuous Dehydration and Open Frameworks", "Zeolites (e.g. Natrolite, Stilbite, Heulandite) are aluminosilicates characterized by framework structures containing large interconnected open channels that permit :", {"A": "Continuous, reversible loss and gain of water (zeolitic water) and rapid cation exchange without collapsing the lattice framework", "B": "Rapid radioactive decay", "C": "Complete transformation into quartz", "D": "Instantaneous dissolution in cold water"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 510–525); Klein & Dutrow (pp. 550–554)"),
    83: ("Polymorphism Groups: Multiplicity of Polymorphs in Carbon and Silica", "Which of the following elemental/chemical groups exhibits the greatest number of natural and synthetic polymorphs in geology ?", {"A": "Silica (SiO2: Quartz, Tridymite, Cristobalite, Coesite, Stishovite, Moganite, Seifertite, Keatite)", "B": "Halite (NaCl)", "C": "Fluorite (CaF2)", "D": "Galena (PbS)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 526–530); Putnis (Mineral Sciences)"),
    84: ("Optical Asterism: Rutile Inclusions in Star Corundum", "'Asterism' (the 6-rayed star optical effect in Star Ruby and Star Sapphire) is caused by incident light scattering from microscopic needle-like inclusions of :", {"A": "Magnetite along octahedral planes", "B": "Zircon along prism faces", "C": "Rutile (TiO2) oriented parallel to the three horizontal crystallographic axes in the basal plane of hexagonal corundum", "D": "Apatite along the c-axis"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 26, 381); Nassau"),
    85: ("Feldspathoid Species: Nepheline, Leucite, Sodalite", "Which of the following minerals is a feldspathoid with the ideal chemical formula KAlSi2O6, characteristically crystallizing in pseudocubic trapezohedral crystals in potassic lavas ?", {"A": "Orthoclase (KAlSi3O8)", "B": "Sanidine", "C": "Anorthoclase", "D": "Leucite (KAlSi2O6)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 490–500); Klein & Dutrow (p. 546)"),
    86: ("Spinel Supergroup: General Formula and Chemistry", "The spinel supergroup minerals possess the general chemical formula AB2O4, where A and B represent :", {"A": "A = monovalent cation, B = trivalent cation", "B": "A = trivalent cation, B = divalent cation", "C": "A = tetravalent cation, B = divalent cation", "D": "A = divalent cation (Mg2+, Fe2+, Zn2+, Mn2+) in tetrahedral sites, B = trivalent cation (Al3+, Fe3+, Cr3+) in octahedral sites"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 400–415); Klein & Dutrow (pp. 388–392)"),
    87: ("Spinel Group Series: Normal vs. Inverse Spinels", "In the normal spinel structure (e.g. MgAl2O4), divalent cations occupy tetrahedral sites and trivalent cations occupy octahedral sites, whereas Magnetite (Fe3O4) is a/an :", {"A": "Normal spinel", "B": "Inverse spinel (Fe3+ in tetrahedral sites, and Fe2+ + Fe3+ disordered in octahedral sites: Fe3+[Fe2+Fe3+]O4)", "C": "Defect tectosilicate", "D": "Orthosilicate"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 390); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    88: ("Corundum Group: Hematite-Corundum Isostructural Oxides", "Corundum (Al2O3) and Hematite (Fe2O3) crystallize in the Trigonal system (R-3c) and are :", {"A": "Isostructural (possessing identical crystal structure and atomic coordination arrangements)", "B": "Polymorphs of each other", "C": "Enantiomorphs", "D": "Isomeric silicates"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 380, 394); Donald E. Sands"),
    89: ("Mica Group Diagnostics: Fuchsite as a Chromium-Bearing Muscovite", "Find the odd man out among the following minerals : Kaolinite, Halloysite, Dickite, Fuchsite :", {"A": "Kaolinite", "B": "Halloysite", "C": "Dickite", "D": "Fuchsite (Fuchsite is a chromian variety of Muscovite mica, whereas the other three are 1:1 clay minerals of the kaolin group)"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *89, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman; Klein & Dutrow"),
    90: ("Metamorphic Rock Assemblages: Ophicalcite Composition", "The decorative metamorphic rock termed 'Ophicalcite' (or Verd Antique) is composed essentially of an intimate crystalline mixture of :", {"A": "Calcite (or Dolomite) and Serpentine (antigorite/chrysotile formed by hydration of forsterite in siliceous dolomitic marble)", "B": "Quartz and Epidote", "C": "Gypsum and Anhydrite", "D": "Corundum and Spinel"}, "Harker (Metamorphism); Deer, Howie & Zussman; Sam Boggs Jr."),
    91: ("Amphibole Quadrilateral: Tremolite-Actinolite-Ferroactinolite", "The calcic amphibole series Tremolite Ca2Mg5Si8O22(OH)2 to Actinolite Ca2(Mg,Fe)5Si8O22(OH)2 represents continuous solid solution involving substitution of :", {"A": "Ca for Na", "B": "Fe2+ for Mg2+", "C": "Al for Si", "D": "Ti for Fe"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 140–155); Klein & Dutrow (p. 476)"),
    92: ("Mica Octahedral Occupancy: Dioctahedral Formulation", "In phyllosilicates, micas are classified as 'dioctahedral' when the number of octahedral cation sites occupied out of every 3 available sites is :", {"A": "1 out of 3", "B": "2 out of 3 (e.g. Muscovite, Paragonite, Margarite, where trivalent Al3+ balances charges using only 2 sites)", "C": "3 out of 3 (trioctahedral)", "D": "All 4 sites"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 490–494); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    93: ("Lithium Micas: Lepidolite and Zinnwaldite", "The diagnostic lilac to rose-pink lithium-bearing mica characteristic of highly differentiated granitic pegmatites is :", {"A": "Biotite", "B": "Lepidolite K(Li,Al)3(Al,Si)4O10(F,OH)2", "C": "Phlogopite", "D": "Muscovite"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 495); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    94: ("Brittle Micas: Margarite as a Calcium-Rich Brittle Mica", "Margarite CaAl2(Al2Si2O10)(OH)2 is termed a 'brittle mica' because its cleavage plates are brittle and non-elastic, which is caused by :", {"A": "Divalent Ca2+ in the interlayer site creating much stronger ionic bonds between tetrahedral sheets than monovalent K+", "B": "Weak hydrogen bonding", "C": "Excess water in interlayer sites", "D": "Absence of aluminum in tetrahedra"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 498); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    95: ("Trioctahedral Mica: Phlogopite as Magnesian Biotite", "The magnesium-rich trioctahedral mica KMg3(AlSi3O10)(OH)2, characteristically formed in metamorphosed siliceous dolomites and kimberlites, is :", {"A": "Muscovite", "B": "Paragonite", "C": "Phlogopite", "D": "Glauconite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 210–220); Klein & Dutrow (p. 493)"),
    96: ("Clay Minerals: Swelling Behavior of Montmorillonite (Smectite)", "The clay mineral group that exhibits spectacular lattice expansion and swelling upon the absorption of polar water molecules into its interlayer space is :", {"A": "Kaolinite (rigid 1:1, non-swelling)", "B": "Smectite / Montmorillonite (2:1 expandable clay)", "C": "Illite (non-swelling 2:1)", "D": "Chlorite"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 508–511); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    97: ("Serpentine Group Polymorphs: Antigorite, Chrysotile, Lizardite", "Antigorite, Lizardite, and Chrysotile share the common trioctahedral 1:1 serpentine chemical formula Mg3Si2O5(OH)4 and are :", {"A": "Polymorphs of serpentine", "B": "Tectosilicates", "C": "Chain silicates", "D": "Framework zeolites"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 344–355); Klein & Dutrow (p. 508)"),
    98: ("Talc Mineralogy: Ultimate Softness on the Mohs Scale", "Talc Mg3Si4O10(OH)2 represents Hardness 1 on the Mohs scale because its 2:1 neutral t-o-t sheets are held together across the interlayer space solely by :", {"A": "Strong covalent bonds", "B": "Extremely weak van der Waals forces (permitting easy slippage of sheets)", "C": "Divalent calcium ionic bonds", "D": "Direct corner-sharing oxygens"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 27, 498); Perkins"),
    99: ("Chlorite Group Structure: 2:1 Layer Plus Brucite-Like Interlayer", "The structure of the chlorite group (Mg,Fe,Al)6(Si,Al)4O10(OH)8 consists of :", {"A": "A single chain of tetrahedra", "B": "Isolated silicate tetrahedra", "C": "Alternating layers of talc and calcite", "D": "A regular alternation of 2:1 trioctahedral mica-like layers with an interlayer brucite-like [Mg(OH)2] octahedral sheet (2:1:1 structure)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 330–342); Klein & Dutrow (p. 500)"),
    100: ("Olivine Thermochemistry: Melting Temperatures of Forsterite vs. Fayalite", "In the olivine binary solid solution system (Mg2SiO4 - Fe2SiO4), Forsterite melts at 1890°C while Fayalite melts at 1205°C because :", {"A": "Fe2+ forms stronger bonds than Mg2+", "B": "Forsterite contains water in its lattice", "C": "Mg2+ (radius 0.66 A) has a significantly smaller ionic radius and higher ionic potential (charge/radius) than Fe2+ (radius 0.74 A), producing much stronger Mg-O lattice bonds and a higher melting point", "D": "Fayalite is a framework silicate"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *100, p. 200); Bowen & Schairer (1935, Am. J. Sci.); Klein & Dutrow (p. 436)"),
    101: ("Garnet Series: End-Member Formulas and Density", "Almandine, the common red-brown metamorphic garnet of metapelitic schists, has the ideal chemical formula :", {"A": "Mg3Al2(SiO4)3 (Pyrope)", "B": "Fe3Al2(SiO4)3 (Almandine)", "C": "Mn3Al2(SiO4)3 (Spessartine)", "D": "Ca3Fe2(SiO4)3 (Andradite)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 20–30); Klein & Dutrow (p. 442)"),
    102: ("Green Garnet Varieties: Uvarovite and Demantoid", "The emerald-green chromian garnet Uvarovite has the stoichiometric composition :", {"A": "Ca3Al2(SiO4)3", "B": "Fe3Al2(SiO4)3", "C": "Ca3Fe2(SiO4)3", "D": "Ca3Cr2(SiO4)3"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 30–35); Klein & Dutrow (p. 443)"),
    103: ("Titanian Garnet: Schorlomite as a Ti-Rich Andradite", "Melanite and Schorlomite are black, titanium-rich varieties of which garnet group member ?", {"A": "Pyrope", "B": "Almandine", "C": "Andradite (Ca3(Fe,Ti)2[(Si,Ti,Fe)O4]3)", "D": "Grossular"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 32–34); Klein & Dutrow (p. 444)"),
    104: ("Grossular Garnet Varieties: Hessonite and Tsavorite", "Hessonite (cinnamon stone) and Tsavorite (vanadian green garnet) are gemstone varieties of :", {"A": "Pyrope", "B": "Almandine", "C": "Grossular (Ca3Al2Si3O12)", "D": "Spessartine"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 443); Webster (Gems)"),
    105: ("Staurolite Crystal Chemistry: Crucial Fe-Al Nesosilicate", "Staurolite Fe2Al9O6(SiO4)4(O,OH)2 characteristically occurs in medium-grade pelitic schists and is structurally composed of alternating slabs of :", {"A": "Kyanite (Al2SiO5) and Iron hydroxide [Fe(OH)2] parallel to (010)", "B": "Garnet and Quartz", "C": "Biotite and Muscovite", "D": "Olivine and Spinel"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 80–86); Klein & Dutrow (p. 450)"),
    106: ("Tourmaline Complex Borosilicate: Polar Asymmetry and Hemimorphism", "Tourmaline Na(Mg,Fe,Li,Al)3Al6(BO3)3Si6O18(OH)4 crystallizes in the Trigonal system in class 3m, which is :", {"A": "Centrosymmetric holohedral", "B": "Enantiomorphic without planes", "C": "Dodecahedral", "D": "Hemimorphic (polar c-axis lacking a horizontal mirror plane or inversion center, exhibiting pyroelectricity and piezoelectricity)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 448–450); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    107: ("Tourmaline Color Varieties: Rubellite, Indicolite, Dravite, Schorl", "Match the diagnostic color variety of Tourmaline with its chemical type :\n(A) Schorl ————— (1) Black iron tourmaline\n(B) Dravite ————— (2) Brown magnesium tourmaline\n(C) Rubellite ———— (3) Pink/red lithium tourmaline\n(D) Indicolite ———— (4) Blue tourmaline", {"A": "A-2, B-1, C-3, D-4", "B": "A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4", "C": "A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1", "D": "A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 449); Webster (Gems)"),
    108: ("Polymorphic Pair Symmetry: Calcite vs. Aragonite", "Which of the following mineral pairs represents polymorphs that crystallize respectively in the Trigonal and Orthorhombic systems ?", {"A": "Orthoclase — Microcline", "B": "Pyrite — Marcasite", "C": "Calcite (Trigonal, R-3c) — Aragonite (Orthorhombic, Pmcn)", "D": "Gypsum — Anhydrite"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *108, p. 200); Klein & Dutrow (pp. 410–415); F.C. Phillips"),
    109: ("Topaz Mineralogy: Nesosilicate with Fluorine and Hydroxyl", "Topaz Al2SiO4(F,OH)2 crystallizes in the Orthorhombic system (2/m 2/m 2/m), possesses a Mohs hardness of 8, and is characterized by :", {"A": "Cubic cleavage", "B": "One direction of highly perfect basal cleavage on {001}", "C": "No cleavage", "D": "Rhombohedral cleavage"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 28, 451); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    110: ("Zircon Geochronology: High U-Pb Retention and Closure Temperature", "Zircon (ZrSiO4) is the premier mineral for U-Pb isotopic geochronology because :", {"A": "It contains abundant lead upon crystallization", "B": "It is water soluble", "C": "It breaks down at 200°C", "D": "It strongly incorporates U4+ and Th4+ into Zr4+ sites while excluding initial Pb2+, has an exceptionally high closure temperature (>900°C), and resists chemical weathering"}, "Faure & Mensing (Isotopes: Principles and Applications); Deer, Howie & Zussman (pp. 45–52)"),
    111: ("Sphene (Titanite): Nesosilicate with Diagnostic Wedge Habit", "Titanite (Sphene) CaTiSiO5 characteristically forms envelope-shaped, sphenoidal (wedge-shaped) monoclinic crystals with very high relief and birefringence in :", {"A": "Intermediate and felsic igneous rocks (granodiorites, diorites, syenites) and calc-silicate metamorphic rocks", "B": "Ultrabasic peridotites only", "C": "Evaporites only", "D": "Coal seams"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 60–66); Klein & Dutrow (p. 454)"),
    112: ("Olivine Crystal Structure: Octahedral Cation Distribution", "In the orthorhombic olivine structure, isolated [SiO4] tetrahedra point alternately up and down along the a-axis, linking cross-shared chains of octahedral M1 and M2 sites occupied by :", {"A": "Ca2+ exclusively", "B": "Al3+ exclusively", "C": "Divalent Mg2+ and Fe2+ in ordered/disordered octahedral coordination", "D": "K+ and Na+"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 3–12); Klein & Dutrow (p. 436)"),
    113: ("Olivine Alteration: Iddingsite and Serpentine", "Under oxidizing hydrothermal and deuteric alteration conditions, olivine commonly alters pseudomorphously along cracks to a reddish-brown, fine-grained mixture of smectite, iron oxyhydroxides (goethite/hematite), and silica termed :", {"A": "Sericite", "B": "Saussurite", "C": "Iddingsite", "D": "Chlorite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 12–15); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    114: ("Serpentinization Reaction: Hydration of Forsterite", "When ultramafic peridotite undergoes hydration (serpentinization) in the presence of silica-bearing hydrothermal fluids, forsterite converts to serpentine according to :", {"A": "3 Mg2SiO4 + SiO2 + 4 H2O -> 2 Mg3Si2O5(OH)4", "B": "Mg2SiO4 + H2O -> MgO + SiO2", "C": "Mg2SiO4 + CO2 -> MgCO3 + SiO2", "D": "3 Mg2SiO4 + SiO2 + 4 H2O -> 2 Mg3Si2O5(OH)4 (Serpentine)"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *115, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman (p. 15); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    115: ("Serpentinization Stoichiometry: Production of Magnetite and Brucite", "In the hydration of iron-bearing olivine (Fo90Fa10) under low-silica reducing conditions, the excess iron that cannot enter the serpentine lattice is precipitated as :", {"A": "Hematite", "B": "Goethite", "C": "Secondary fine-grained Magnetite (Fe3O4) and Brucite [Mg(OH)2], releasing hydrogen gas (H2)", "D": "Pyrite"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *115, p. 200); Klein & Dutrow (p. 508); Frost & Beard (2007)"),
    116: ("Pyroxene Quad: Wollastonite Immiscibility Gap", "In the ternary CaSiO3 - MgSiO3 - FeSiO3 pyroxene quadrilateral, pure Wollastonite (CaSiO3) does not form pyroxene because :", {"A": "Ca2+ is too large (radius 1.00 A) to fit into pyroxene octahedral M2 sites without restructuring the chain repeat into a 3-tetrahedron pyroxenoid chain", "B": "Silicon cannot bond with calcium", "C": "Oxygen ions are too small", "D": "Calcium has a charge of +3"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 105–112); Klein & Dutrow (p. 460)"),
    117: ("Orthopyroxene Inversion: Pigeonite vs. Enstatite", "In slowly cooled gabbros and norites, high-temperature calcic clinopyroxene Pigeonite inverts subsolidus to Orthopyroxene with exsolution of :", {"A": "Quartz", "B": "Biotite", "C": "Feldspar", "D": "Augite lamellae parallel to (001)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Introduction to the Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 118–122); Klein & Dutrow (p. 464)"),
    118: ("Clinopyroxene: Diagnostic Optical and Crystallographic Symmetry of Augite", "Augite (Ca,Na)(Mg,Fe,Al,Ti)(Si,Al)2O6 crystallizes in the Monoclinic system (space group C2/c) and is characterized optically in thin sections by :", {"A": "Parallel extinction and low relief", "B": "Isotropic character", "C": "High relief, inclined extinction (Z ^ c = 36° to 48°), and two sets of prismatic cleavage at ~87°/93°", "D": "Uniaxial negative sign"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 115–125); Nesse (Optical Mineralogy)"),
    119: ("Sodium Pyroxenes: Jadeite and Aegirine (Acmite)", "The sodic pyroxene characteristic of alkali igneous rocks (nepheline syenites, phonolites) with the ideal formula NaFe3+Si2O6 is :", {"A": "Jadeite (NaAlSi2O6)", "B": "Aegirine / Acmite", "C": "Omphacite", "D": "Hedenbergite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 128–135); Klein & Dutrow (p. 466)"),
    120: ("High-Pressure Pyroxene: Omphacite in Eclogite Facies", "The diagnostic sodic-calcic clinopyroxene formed exclusively under high-pressure, low-temperature metamorphic conditions in eclogites is :", {"A": "Diopside", "B": "Augite", "C": "Spodumene", "D": "Omphacite ((Ca,Na)(Mg,Fe2+,Fe3+,Al)Si2O6)"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *120, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman (pp. 125–130); Klein & Dutrow (p. 465)")
}

# Part 2B: Q121 to Q180
REMAINING_QS_P2 = {
    121: ("Lithium Pyroxene: Spodumene as Major Lithium Ore", "The lithium-bearing pyroxene LiAlSi2O6, characteristically found in giant monoclinic crystals in complex granitic pegmatites, is :", {"A": "Spodumene", "B": "Lepidolite", "C": "Petalite", "D": "Amblygonite"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 467); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    122: ("Pyroxenoid Minerals: Rhodonite and Pyroxmangite", "Rhodonite (MnSiO3) is a pyroxenoid mineral with a repeating single-chain unit of :", {"A": "2 tetrahedra", "B": "5 tetrahedra ([Mn5Si5O15], repeat distance ~12.2 A)", "C": "3 tetrahedra", "D": "7 tetrahedra"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 470–472); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    123: ("Amphibole End-Members: Anthophyllite and Gedrite", "Anthophyllite (Mg,Fe)7Si8O22(OH)2 is an orthorhombic amphibole (orthoamphibole) characteristic of :", {"A": "Metamorphosed ultramafic rocks and cordierite-anthophyllite schists", "B": "Alkali granites only", "C": "Carbonatites only", "D": "Limestones"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 138–142); Klein & Dutrow (p. 476)"),
    124: ("Blue Amphibole: Glaucophane and Riebeckite", "The sodic blue amphibole diagnostic of high-pressure/low-temperature subduction zone metamorphism (Blueschist facies) with formula Na2(Mg3Al2)Si8O22(OH)2 is :", {"A": "Hornblende", "B": "Actinolite", "C": "Tremolite", "D": "Glaucophane"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 150–160); Klein & Dutrow (p. 480)"),
    125: ("Pigment Minerals: Lithopone Composition", "The industrial white pigment 'Lithopone' is manufactured by co-precipitating :", {"A": "Calcite and Gypsum", "B": "Barium sulfate (Barite, BaSO4) and Zinc sulfide (Sphalerite, ZnS)", "C": "Titanium dioxide and Silica", "D": "Lead carbonate and Kaolinite"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Official Key, p. 199); Industrial Minerals and Rocks; Klein & Dutrow"),
    126: ("Feldspar End-Members: Ternary Feldspar System", "The three fundamental components of the ternary rock-forming feldspar system are :", {"A": "Quartz, Corundum, Hematite", "B": "Calcite, Dolomite, Magnesite", "C": "Orthoclase (KAlSi3O8), Albite (NaAlSi3O8), Anorthite (CaAl2Si2O8)", "D": "Nepheline, Kalsilite, Leucite"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 530–535); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    127: ("Epidote Group Varieties: Withamite Chemistry", "Which of the following epidote group minerals is NOT a chromium-bearing mineral but rather a manganese/iron-bearing variety of clinozoisite/epidote ?", {"A": "Uvarovite (Cr-garnet)", "B": "Fuchsite (Cr-mica)", "C": "Chrome-diopside", "D": "Withamite (a piedmontite/epidote variety containing Mn3+ and Fe3+, free of essential Cr)"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *127, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman (pp. 82–88); Dana's Mineralogy"),
    128: ("Plagioclase Subdivisions: Six Compositional Ranges", "The universally accepted classification of the Plagioclase feldspar series into six mineral species based on mole % Anorthite (An) is :\nAlbite (An 0-10), Oligoclase (An 10-30), Andesine (An 30-50), Labradorite (An 50-70), Bytownite (An 70-90), Anorthite (An 90-100)\nIn this series, Labradorite corresponds to :", {"A": "An 10–30", "B": "An 50–70 (famously displaying schiller labradorescence)", "C": "An 30–50", "D": "An 70–90"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 380–395); Klein & Dutrow (p. 534)"),
    129: ("Plagioclase Optical Textures: Labradorescence and Boggild Gap", "The brilliant blue-green iridescent play of color (labradorescence) observed in gem labradorite is caused by submicroscopic exsolution lamellae within the :", {"A": "Peristerite gap (An 2–16)", "B": "Huttenlocher gap (An 65–90)", "C": "Carlsbad twin boundary", "D": "Bøggild miscibility gap (An 48–58)"}, "Smith & Brown (Feldspar Minerals, Vol. 1); Deer, Howie & Zussman (p. 388); Klein & Dutrow (p. 536)"),
    130: ("Alkali Feldspar Textures: Perthite vs. Antiperthite", "When exsolution lamellae of sodic plagioclase (Albite) occur hosted within a dominant potassium feldspar (Microcline/Orthoclase) host crystal, the texture is termed :", {"A": "Myrmekite", "B": "Graphic texture", "C": "Perthite (antiperthite is K-feldspar lamellae in plagioclase host)", "D": "Symplectite"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *130, p. 200); Klein & Dutrow (p. 536); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    131: ("Myrmekite Texture: Intergrowth of Quartz and Plagioclase", "The vermicular (worm-like) intergrowth of quartz rods enclosed within sodic plagioclase feldspar encroaching on K-feldspar is termed :", {"A": "Myrmekite", "B": "Perthite", "C": "Poikilitic texture", "D": "Coronitic texture"}, "Phillips (1974, Myrmekite and related textures); Deer, Howie & Zussman; Sam Boggs Jr."),
    132: ("Graphic Granite: Runite Intergrowth of Quartz and Microcline", "The macroscopic intergrowth of skeletal quartz crystals resembling ancient cuneiform or Arabic writing hosted inside potassium feldspar (microcline) is termed :", {"A": "Granophyric texture", "B": "Orbicular texture", "C": "Graphic granite (Runite)", "D": "Spinifex texture"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, p. 410); Sam Boggs Jr.; Klein & Dutrow"),
    133: ("Zeolite Group Cation Exchange: Molecular Sieve Applications", "Zeolite minerals are utilized extensively in industry as water softeners, catalysts, and molecular sieves because their open aluminosilicate cage frameworks allow :", {"A": "Radioactive fusion", "B": "Dissolution into liquid petroleum", "C": "Rapid, selective, reversible cation exchange (e.g. trading Na+ in the zeolite for Ca2+/Mg2+ in hard water)", "D": "Permanent shrinkage"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 550–554); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    134: ("Silica Varieties: Cryptocrystalline Chalcedony and Agate", "Fibrous, microscopic, cryptocrystalline varieties of silica (SiO2) characterized by concentric multicolored banding are classified as :", {"A": "Agate (banded variety of Chalcedony)", "B": "Rock crystal (coarse macroscopic quartz)", "C": "Flint", "D": "Jasper"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 528–530); Perkins"),
    135: ("Calcite Morphological Varieties: Dogtooth Spar", "The morphological variety of Calcite that characteristically crystallizes in sharp, pointed scalenohedral crystals resembling canine teeth is termed :", {"A": "Nail-head spar (flat rhombohedral crystals)", "B": "Iceland spar (optical transparent cleavage rhombohedra)", "C": "Satin spar (fibrous gypsum/calcite)", "D": "Dogtooth spar (scalenohedral calcite crystals)"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *135, p. 200); Klein & Dutrow (p. 411); Dana's Mineralogy"),
    136: ("Calcite Optical Gemology: Iceland Spar Birefringence", "'Iceland spar' is historically famous in physical optics and petrography because it displays :", {"A": "Extraordinary double refraction (birefringence delta = 0.172), producing clean, widely separated double images", "B": "High radioactivity", "C": "Complete opacity", "D": "Extreme hardness (H = 9)"}, "Erasmus Bartholin (1669, Experimenta crystalli islandici); Klein & Dutrow (p. 411)"),
    137: ("Aragonite Group Minerals: Orthorhombic Carbonates", "The Aragonite isostructural group comprises carbonates of divalent cations with ionic radii > 1.0 A, crystallizing in the Orthorhombic system, including :", {"A": "Aragonite (CaCO3), Witherite (BaCO3), Strontianite (SrCO3), Cerussite (PbCO3)", "B": "Calcite, Siderite, Magnesite, Rhodochrosite", "C": "Dolomite, Ankerite, Kutnohorite", "D": "Malachite, Azurite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 315–325); Klein & Dutrow (p. 415)"),
    138: ("Calcite Group Minerals: Trigonal Carbonates", "The Calcite isostructural group comprises carbonates of smaller divalent cations (radii < 1.0 A), crystallizing in the Trigonal system (R-3c), including :", {"A": "Witherite, Strontianite, Cerussite", "B": "Aragonite, Barite, Celestite", "C": "Calcite (CaCO3), Magnesite (MgCO3), Siderite (FeCO3), Rhodochrosite (MnCO3), Smithsonite (ZnCO3)", "D": "Gypsum, Anhydrite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 305–315); Klein & Dutrow (p. 410)"),
    139: ("Sulfate Group Mineralogy: Gypsum and Anhydrite Relationships", "Gypsum (CaSO4.2H2O) dehydrates at elevated temperatures and pressures to form Anhydrite (CaSO4), resulting in a dramatic solid volume reduction of approximately :", {"A": "5%", "B": "15%", "C": "39% to 42% volume loss (with density increasing from 2.32 to 2.96 g/cm3)", "D": "80%"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 450–458); Sam Boggs Jr.; Klein & Dutrow"),
    140: ("Barite Group Mineralogy: Isostructural Orthorhombic Sulfates", "Barite (BaSO4), Celestite (SrSO4), and Anglesite (PbSO4) form the Barite isostructural group, which crystallizes in the :", {"A": "Orthorhombic system (space group Pnma)", "B": "Monoclinic system", "C": "Cubic system", "D": "Tetragonal system"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 418–422); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    141: ("Phosphate Minerals: Apatite Supergroup Structure", "Apatite Ca5(PO4)3(F,Cl,OH) is the primary reservoir of phosphorus in the Earth's crust and crystallizes in the :", {"A": "Cubic system", "B": "Orthorhombic system", "C": "Monoclinic system", "D": "Hexagonal system (class 6/m, space group P6_3/m)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 424–428); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    142: ("Rare Earth Phosphates: Monazite and Xenotime", "Monazite (Ce,La,Nd,Th)PO4 and Xenotime (YPO4) are crucial economic sources of Rare Earth Elements (REE) and crystallize respectively in the :", {"A": "Both in Cubic system", "B": "Both in Hexagonal system", "C": "Monazite (Monoclinic: light REE) and Xenotime (Tetragonal: heavy REE)", "D": "Both in Triclinic system"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 440–448); Klein & Dutrow"),
    143: ("Halide Minerals: Rock Salt (Halite) Structure", "In the Halite (NaCl) crystal structure (space group Fm-3m), each Na+ cation is surrounded by 6 Cl- anions in octahedral coordination, and each Cl- anion is surrounded by :", {"A": "6 Na+ cations in octahedral coordination (CN = 6:6)", "B": "4 Na+ cations", "C": "8 Na+ cations", "D": "12 Na+ cations"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 370–374); Donald E. Sands"),
    144: ("Fluorite Structure: 8:4 Coordination Polyhedra", "In the Fluorite (CaF2) crystal structure (space group Fm-3m), each Ca2+ cation is in 8-fold cubic coordination with fluorine, and each F- anion is in :", {"A": "8-fold cubic coordination", "B": "6-fold octahedral coordination", "C": "4-fold tetrahedral coordination (CN = 8:4)", "D": "12-fold coordination"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 374–376); Donald E. Sands"),
    145: ("Native Elements: Gold-Silver Complete Solid Solution", "Native Gold (Au) and Native Silver (Ag) form a complete, continuous solid solution series at all temperatures because :", {"A": "Au (radius 1.44 A) and Ag (radius 1.44 A) have virtually identical metallic radii due to the Lanthanide Contraction, and both share the Face-Centered Cubic (FCC) lattice", "B": "They are covalent liquids", "C": "They are silicates", "D": "They have identical boiling points"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 334–338); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    146: ("Sulfide Mineralogy: Pyrite vs. Marcasite Dimorphism", "Pyrite (FeS2) and Marcasite (FeS2) are classical dimorphs of iron disulfide that crystallize respectively in the :", {"A": "Tetragonal and Hexagonal systems", "B": "Both in Orthorhombic system", "C": "Pyrite (Isometric, class 2/m 3) and Marcasite (Orthorhombic, class 2/m 2/m 2/m)", "D": "Monoclinic and Triclinic systems"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 362–366); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    147: ("Quartz Varieties: Optical Grade Rock Crystal", "The purest, perfectly transparent, completely colorless macroscopic variety of quartz, completely free from inclusions and fractures, is termed :", {"A": "Milky quartz", "B": "Smoky quartz", "C": "Rock crystal", "D": "Rose quartz"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 528); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    148: ("Silica Mineraloid: Amorphous Nature of Opal", "Opal (SiO2.nH2O) is scientifically classified as a mineraloid rather than a true mineral because :", {"A": "It lacks an orderly internal three-dimensional periodic crystalline lattice, consisting instead of close-packed amorphous silica spheres with interstitial water", "B": "It has no chemical formula", "C": "It is an organic compound", "D": "It dissolves in cold rainwater"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 529–530); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    149: ("Feldspathoid Stability: Reaction with Free Silica", "When Nepheline (NaAlSiO4) is brought into contact with excess free silica (SiO2) under magmatic conditions, it reacts quantitatively to form :", {"A": "Corundum", "B": "Jadeite", "C": "Albite feldspar (NaAlSiO4 + 2 SiO2 -> NaAlSi3O8)", "D": "Analcime"}, "Bowen (The Evolution of the Igneous Rocks); Deer, Howie & Zussman; Klein & Dutrow"),
    150: ("Banded Chalcedony: Onyx vs. Sardonyx", "A parallel, flat-banded cryptocrystalline variety of chalcedony with alternating stark white and jet-black layers is termed :", {"A": "Carnelian", "B": "Bloodstone (Heliotrope)", "C": "Onyx (sardonyx has brown/red and white bands)", "D": "Chrysoprase"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, p. 529); Webster (Gems)"),
    151: ("Feldspar Ordering: Al-Si Distribution in Orthoclase vs. Sanidine", "The structural state of potassium feldspar (KAlSi3O8) evolves during cooling such that Al and Si atoms, which are randomly distributed across tetrahedral sites in Sanidine, become :", {"A": "Completely expelled from the lattice", "B": "Progressively ordered into specific T1 sites (leading to monoclinic Orthoclase and finally fully ordered triclinic Microcline)", "C": "Replaced by iron and magnesium", "D": "Converted into isolated tetrahedra"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 395–410); Klein & Dutrow (pp. 532–536)"),
    152: ("Feldspar Ordering Symmetry: The Principle of Symmetrical Degradation", "As the degree of Si/Al tetrahedral ordering in potassium feldspar increases during slow cooling from Sanidine to Microcline :", {"A": "The crystal symmetry increases from monoclinic to cubic", "B": "The crystal symmetry remains invariant", "C": "The crystal symmetry is degraded/lowered from Monoclinic (2/m in high sanidine) to Triclinic (-1 in fully ordered maximum microcline)", "D": "The mineral becomes amorphous"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *153, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman; Klein & Dutrow"),
    153: ("Ordering Kinetics: High Symmetry in Disordered States", "In mineral solid solutions and order-disorder transformations, the general crystallographic rule relating internal atomic ordering to external point group symmetry is :", {"A": "The greater the atomic ordering, the lower is the symmetry (disordered high-T phases display higher symmetry)", "B": "The greater the ordering, the higher is the symmetry", "C": "Ordering has no effect on symmetry", "D": "Ordering destroys all symmetry elements"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *153, p. 200); Donald E. Sands; Putnis (Mineral Sciences)"),
    154: ("Barium Feldspars: Celsian and Hyalophane", "The rare barium-rich end-member of the feldspar group with the ideal chemical formula BaAl2Si2O8 is :", {"A": "Celsian (hyalophane is a (K,Ba) intermediate barium feldspar)", "B": "Buddingtonite (ammonium feldspar)", "C": "Bavenite", "D": "Danburite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 415–420); Klein & Dutrow (p. 535)"),
    155: ("Feldspar Twinning Laws: Manebach vs. Baveno Laws", "In monoclinic and triclinic feldspars, contact twinning on the basal pinacoid {001} is known as the :", {"A": "Manebach twin law", "B": "Baveno twin law ({021})", "C": "Carlsbad twin law", "D": "Albite twin law"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 193, 538); F.C. Phillips"),
    156: ("Pericline Twinning: Rhombic Section and Axis of Rotation", "In plagioclase feldspars, the Pericline twin law is defined by a 180° rotation around the :", {"A": "c-axis [001]", "B": "a-axis [100]", "C": "Perpendicular to (010)", "D": "b-axis [010] (with the composition plane being the rhombic section)", "E": "None of the above"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 193, 538); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    157: ("Pyroxene Quad: Diopside-Hedenbergite Solid Solution", "The continuous solid-solution series between Diopside CaMgSi2O6 and Hedenbergite CaFeSi2O6 involves substitution of :", {"A": "Ca for Mg", "B": "Fe2+ for Mg2+ in the M1 octahedral site with Ca fixed in the M2 site", "C": "Al for Si", "D": "Na for Ca"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 110–118); Klein & Dutrow (p. 462)"),
    158: ("Pyroxene Structure: Space Group Symmetry of Clinopyroxenes", "The dominant space group symmetry of normal calcic clinopyroxenes (Diopside, Hedenbergite, Augite) is :", {"A": "P2_1/c (low-Ca clinopyroxene/pigeonite)", "B": "Pbca (orthopyroxene)", "C": "Pccn", "D": "C2/c (monoclinic base-centered space group)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 105–115); Klein & Dutrow (p. 460)"),
    159: ("Orthopyroxene End-Members: Enstatite-Ferrosilite", "The pure magnesium and pure iron end-members of the orthopyroxene solid-solution series (Pbca) are respectively :", {"A": "Diopside and Hedenbergite", "B": "Enstatite (Mg2Si2O6) and Ferrosilite (Fe2Si2O6)", "C": "Forsterite and Fayalite", "D": "Anthophyllite and Cummingtonite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 105–112); Klein & Dutrow (p. 462)"),
    160: ("Intermediate Orthopyroxenes: Bronzite and Hypersthene", "In classical mineralogy, orthopyroxenes containing 10% to 30% ferrosilite component (En90Fs10 to En70Fs30) famously displaying bronze-like schiller luster are termed :", {"A": "Pigeonite", "B": "Bronzite (Hypersthene is En70Fs30 to En50Fs50)", "C": "Augite", "D": "Diallage"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Introduction to the Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 108–112); Klein & Dutrow"),
    161: ("Pyroxene Cleavage Mechanics: Trans-Chain Fracture Geometry", "Pyroxenes exhibit two directions of prismatic cleavage intersecting at ~87° and 93° because cleavage fractures propagate :", {"A": "Between the interlocking octahedral-tetrahedral I-beams parallel to the c-axis without severing strong intra-chain Si-O bonds", "B": "Directly cutting across silicon tetrahedra", "C": "Along basal pinacoidal planes", "D": "Only along twin planes"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 458–460); Donald E. Sands"),
    162: ("High-Pressure Pyroxene: Jadeite Stability with Quartz", "Jadeite (NaAlSi2O6) forms at high metamorphic pressures in subduction complexes via the subsolidus reaction of Albite breaking down into :", {"A": "Nepheline + K-feldspar", "B": "Jadeite + Quartz (NaAlSi3O8 -> NaAlSi2O6 + SiO2)", "C": "Corundum + Sillimanite", "D": "Analcime + Kaolinite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 130–135); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    163: ("Pyroxene Jadeite Intermediate: Jadeite between Albite and Nepheline", "Chemically, the sodic clinopyroxene Jadeite (NaAlSi2O6) is intermediate in composition between :", {"A": "Diopside and Augite", "B": "Quartz and Corundum", "C": "Albite (NaAlSi3O8) and Nepheline (NaAlSiO4)", "D": "Enstatite and Ferrosilite"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, p. 132); Sam Boggs Jr.; Klein & Dutrow"),
    164: ("Augite Crystal Chemistry: Aluminum in Both 4- and 6-Coordination", "In the complex clinopyroxene Augite (Ca,Na)(Mg,Fe,Al,Ti)(Si,Al)2O6, aluminum ions (Al3+) uniquely reside in :", {"A": "Only tetrahedral sites substituting for Si4+", "B": "Only octahedral sites substituting for Mg2+/Fe2+", "C": "Both 4-fold tetrahedral coordination (substituting for Si4+) and 6-fold octahedral coordination (substituting for Mg2+/Fe2+ in the M1 site)", "D": "Interlayer sites"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 115–125); Klein & Dutrow (p. 464)"),
    165: ("Pyroxene Diallage Texture: Parting along (100)", "The pyroxene variety known as 'Diallage' is a modified augite or diopside characterized by a distinctive metallic platy parting parallel to :", {"A": "(001) only", "B": "(100) plane (often induced by exsolution lamellae of pigeonite or orthopyroxene)", "C": "(010)", "D": "(111)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, p. 116); Dana's Mineralogy"),
    166: ("Pyroxene Quadrilateral Immiscibility: Augite-Pigeonite Miscibility Gap", "In basaltic magma crystallization, the co-crystallization of two distinct pyroxenes (Ca-rich Augite and Ca-poor Pigeonite or Orthopyroxene) is direct evidence of a/an :", {"A": "Complete continuous solid solution", "B": "Eutectic absence of crystallization", "C": "Peritectic dissolution", "D": "Immiscibility gap (solvus) between high-Ca clinopyroxene and low-Ca pyroxene"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 105–120); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    167: ("Pyroxene Chain Stoichiometry: Silicon to Oxygen Ratio", "In the inosilicate single chain structure of pyroxenes, the fundamental stoichiometric ratio of Silicon to Oxygen (Si:O) is :", {"A": "1 : 4 (Nesosilicates)", "B": "2 : 7 (Sorosilicates)", "C": "4 : 11 (Amphiboles)", "D": "1 : 3 (Inosilicates single-chain: [Si2O6]4- or [SiO3]2-)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 458–460); F.C. Phillips"),
    168: ("Amphibole Stoichiometry: Silicon to Oxygen Ratio", "In the inosilicate double chain structure of amphiboles, the fundamental stoichiometric ratio of Silicon to Oxygen (Si:O) is :", {"A": "1 : 3", "B": "2 : 5 (Phyllosilicates)", "C": "4 : 11 ([Si4O11]6- or [Si8O22]12-)", "D": "1 : 2 (Tectosilicates)"}, "Klein & Dutrow (Manual of Mineral Science, pp. 474–478); Deer, Howie & Zussman"),
    169: ("Potassium Feldspar Polymorphs: Microcline vs. Orthoclase", "The diagnostic optical feature that uniquely identifies Microcline from Orthoclase under cross-polarized light in petrographic thin sections is :", {"A": "Straight extinction", "B": "Cross-hatched 'tartan' or 'gridiron' twinning (combined Albite and Pericline laws)", "C": "Uniaxial negative interference figure", "D": "High double refraction"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Introduction to the Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 405–410); Nesse"),
    170: ("Plagioclase Feldspar Zoning: Normal vs. Reverse Zoning", "In igneous petrology, 'Normal Zoning' in plagioclase phenocrysts is defined as a continuous variation from :", {"A": "Sodic rim to sodic core", "B": "Potassic core to sodic rim", "C": "Calcic/anorthite-rich core to a progressively more sodic/albite-rich rim", "D": "Albite core to anorthite rim"}, "Sam Boggs Jr. (Principles of Sedimentology and Stratigraphy); Deer, Howie & Zussman (p. 390)"),
    171: ("Reverse Plagioclase Zoning: Thermal or Baric Resurgence", "'Reverse Zoning' in plagioclase crystals, where the crystal core is more sodic and the outer rim is progressively more calcic (anorthite-rich), typically indicates :", {"A": "Continuous equilibrium cooling without disturbance", "B": "Complete fractional crystallization", "C": "Magma mixing, sudden increase in temperature, or rapid decompression in the magma chamber", "D": "Post-magmatic chemical weathering"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, p. 391); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    172: ("Oscillatory Plagioclase Zoning: Diffusion-Controlled Growth", "Phenocrysts displaying fine concentric shells with alternating calcic and sodic compositions are described as exhibiting :", {"A": "Sector zoning", "B": "Oscillatory zoning (produced by localized kinetic diffusion boundary layers at the crystal-melt interface)", "C": "Undulose zoning", "D": "Hourglass zoning"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, p. 392); Sam Boggs Jr."),
    173: ("Hourglass Zoning: Sector Growth in Titanaugite", "The distinctive 'Hourglass structure' observed in petrographic thin sections of titanaugite clinopyroxene is a classic manifestation of :", {"A": "Sector zoning (differential incorporation of Ti and Al on different growing crystal faces {100} vs {010})", "B": "Polysynthetic twinning", "C": "Exsolution lamellae", "D": "Subsolidus deformation"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 116–118); Nesse"),
    174: ("Scapolite Series: Marialite and Meionite End-Members", "The scapolite mineral series forms a solid solution between the sodic end-member Marialite Na4Al3Si9O24Cl and the calcic end-member Meionite :", {"A": "Ca4Al6Si6O24(CO3,SO4)", "B": "Ca3Al2Si3O12", "C": "K4Al3Si9O24Cl", "D": "Ca4Al6Si6O24CO3 (Meionite)"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 480–488); Klein & Dutrow (p. 548)"),
    175: ("Scapolite Structure: Resemblance to Plagioclase and Volatile Trapping", "Scapolites resemble plagioclase feldspars in composition and metamorphic petrogenesis, but their open framework contains large structural cages that accommodate volatile anions such as :", {"A": "Only H2O molecules", "B": "Only noble gases", "C": "Cl-, CO3^2-, and SO4^2- anions (acting as geological 'double salts' of plagioclase)", "D": "Phosphate groups"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *179, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman (p. 480); Klein & Dutrow"),
    176: ("Zeolite Cation Exchange: Structural Channel Geometry", "The exceptional ability of Zeolite group minerals to perform reversible base-exchange (exchanging heavy metals and alkali cations) is directly facilitated by their :", {"A": "Open, rigid, continuous three-dimensional tetrahedral framework intersected by large channels and cages of dimensions 3 to 10 Angstroms", "B": "Isolated silicate tetrahedra", "C": "Single chain structures", "D": "Sheet structures"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 510–525); Klein & Dutrow (p. 550)"),
    177: ("Cancrinite and Sodalite: Feldspathoids with Trapped Anions", "Sodalite Na8(AlSiO4)6Cl2 and Cancrinite Na6Ca2(AlSiO4)6(CO3)2.2H2O are feldspathoids distinguished by possessing :", {"A": "Cages within their framework structures that enclose large interstitial anions (Cl- in sodalite, CO3^2- and H2O in cancrinite)", "B": "Isolated nesosilicate tetrahedra", "C": "Excessive free quartz", "D": "Mica sheets"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 500–508); Klein & Dutrow (p. 547)"),
    178: ("Garnet Chemical Formula: General Stoichiometric Framework", "In the general formula of the garnet group X3Y2(SiO4)3, the divalent X-cations (Ca, Mg, Fe2+, Mn) reside in 8-fold dodecahedral coordination, while the trivalent Y-cations (Al, Fe3+, Cr) reside in :", {"A": "4-fold tetrahedral sites", "B": "6-fold octahedral coordination sites", "C": "12-fold cuboctahedral sites", "D": "Interstitial planar sites"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 20–25); Klein & Dutrow (p. 441)"),
    179: ("Scapolite as Double Salts: Geochemical Framework Analog", "In structural mineralogy, scapolites are scientifically described as 'double salts' of plagioclase feldspars with salts such as NaCl, CaCO3, or CaSO4 because :", {"A": "3 Marialite = 3 Albite + NaCl, and 3 Meionite = 3 Anorthite + CaCO3", "B": "They contain double the amount of silicon", "C": "They are composed of two identical polymorphs", "D": "They crystallize in two crystal classes simultaneously"}, "Dr. Ausaf Sayeed (Explanatory Note *179, p. 200); Deer, Howie & Zussman (p. 480); Dana's Mineralogy"),
    180: ("Titanium Garnet: Schorlomite as Titanium-Rich Andradite", "'Schorlomite' is officially recognized in mineralogy as a black, opaque, titanium-rich variety of :", {"A": "Pyrope", "B": "Grossular", "C": "Andradite garnet (Ca3(Fe3+,Ti)2[(Si,Ti,Fe)O4]3, containing up to 15-20 wt% TiO2)", "D": "Almandine"}, "Deer, Howie & Zussman (Rock-Forming Minerals, pp. 32–35); Klein & Dutrow (p. 444); Dana's System of Mineralogy")
}

